single-color divider, in either orientation, identify the panels and align corresponding coordinates. Determine the color common to both panels (it may be a dominant background in one or both panels, or a marker in one panel; do not assume it is a foreground marker in both). Output one panel-sized grid, omitting the divider, filled with that common color. Place 3 only where both aligned input cells equal the common color. Do not mark the union of marker cells or a cell where the common color appears in only one panel. Preserve dimensions and validate panel alignment and row widths. Important parsing safeguard: count the divider exactly once, split into two equal panels, and compare row/column index i to index i; especially audit the final output rows on narrow grids to prevent a one-row offset. Demonstrated across horizontal and vertical dividers, with shared colors that are backgrounds or markers: examples use output backgrounds 2, 4, 6, 7, and 0, always marking only coordinate-wise overlap of the shared color with 3.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:08:55.865Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:16:07.978326+00:00","version":5},"after":{"id":"arc_overlap_shared_color_across_divided_panels","kind":"memory","title":"Mark coordinate-wise shared-color overlap across divided panels","content":"For ARC inputs consisting of two equal-sized panels separated by a single-color divider, in either orientation, identify the panels and remove exactly the divider. Find a color present in both panels; it may be a background or a sparse marker, including when the panel backgrounds differ. Align the panels by corresponding coordinates and produce one panel-sized grid initialized to the shared color. Replace a coordinate with 3 only when that color occurs in both panels at that aligned coordinate. If there are no aligned occurrences, return the uniform shared-color grid. Validate panel dimensions, row widths, divider parsing, and alignment before submitting. Validated examples include panels with different backgrounds (8 and 7) whose only shared color is sparse marker 1; when no marker coordinates align, the correct output is all 1.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:08:55.865Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:16:40.490Z","version":6},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929122002262","summary":"Strengthen the global divided-panel overlap memory with validated color-selection, coordinate-alignment, and parsing safeguards.","rationale":"The demonstrations and solved test confirm that the shared color can be a background in one panel and a sparse mark in the other, and that 3 marks only aligned cells where both panels contain that color. Repeated incorrect submissions resulted from panel-indexing errors.","expectedOutcome":"Future matching tasks should identify the common panel color, compare exact local coordinates after removing the divider, and validate the output shape and marked cells before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_overlap_shared_color_across_divided_panels","title":"Mark coordinate-wise shared-color overlap across divided panels","content":"For ARC inputs with two equal-sized panels separated by a single-color divider, in either orientation, identify the panels and remove exactly the divider. Find the color present in both panels; it may be a background in one panel and a sparse mark in the other. Create one panel-sized output filled with that shared color, and put 3 only at coordinates where both panels contain the shared color at the same aligned local coordinate. Do not mark occurrences found in only one panel. For a vertical split, the left panel has columns before the divider and the right panel begins immediately after it; compare columns using local panel coordinates, not input-grid coordinates. Parse and count panel entries explicitly before comparing. Enumerate each panel's coordinates containing the shared color, intersect those coordinate sets, then validate output dimensions and every 3 position. A validated vertical-split case exposed a one-column transcription error: a right-panel marker in row 1 was at local column 10, not 9.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved instance and demonstrations validate the common-color overlap rule and show that explicit panel indexing and coordinate-set intersection help prevent repeated transcription errors.","before":{"id":"arc_overlap_shared_color_across_divided_panels","kind":"memory","title":"Mark coordinate-wise shared-color overlap across divided panels","content":"For ARC inputs consisting of two equal-sized panels separated by a single-color divider, in either orientation, identify the panels and remove exactly the divider. Find a color present in both panels; it may be a background or a sparse marker, including when the panel backgrounds differ. Align the panels by corresponding coordinates and produce one panel-sized grid initialized to the shared color. Replace a coordinate with 3 only when that color occurs in both panels at that aligned coordinate. If there are no aligned occurrences, return the uniform shared-color grid. Validate panel dimensions, row widths, divider parsing, and alignment before submitting. Validated examples include panels with different backgrounds (8 and 7) whose only shared color is sparse marker 1; when no marker coordinates align, the correct output is all 1.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:08:55.865Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:16:40.490Z","version":6},"after":{"id":"arc_overlap_shared_color_across_divided_panels","kind":"memory","title":"Mark coordinate-wise shared-color overlap across divided panels","content":"For ARC inputs with two equal-sized panels separated by a single-color divider, in either orientation, identify the panels and remove exactly the divider. Find the color present in both panels; it may be a background in one panel and a sparse mark in the other. Create one panel-sized output filled with that shared color, and put 3 only at coordinates where both panels contain the shared color at the same aligned local coordinate. Do not mark occurrences found in only one panel. For a vertical split, the left panel has columns before the divider and the right panel begins immediately after it; compare columns using local panel coordinates, not input-grid coordinates. Parse and count panel entries explicitly before comparing. Enumerate each panel's coordinates containing the shared color, intersect those coordinate sets, then validate output dimensions and every 3 position. A validated vertical-split case exposed a one-column transcription error: a right-panel marker in row 1 was at local column 10, not 9.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:08:55.865Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:20:09.151Z","version":7},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929125711104","summary":"Updated the global diagonal-stripe memory with the validated period-3 case and a safeguard to infer the smallest consistent period from all observed stripes.","rationale":"The task confirmed that down-right stripes use the invariant c-r: the observed stripes at 6, 16, and -7 map modulo 3 to colors 5, 1, and 1. An earlier period-2 guess was incorrect; using all observed stripes yielded the correct dense grid.","expectedOutcome":"Future matching tasks should use every distinct observed stripe and color to identify the smallest consistent period, then fill the grid by residue; with insufficient stripe evidence, consider requesting a demonstration.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_periodic_diagonal_stripe_patterns_across_the_grid","title":"ARC: extend periodic diagonal stripe patterns across the grid","content":"For ARC grids whose nonzero cells lie on one or more complete or partial parallel diagonal lines and whose output is a dense periodic pattern, identify the line-family invariant: c-r for down-right diagonals, or r+c for down-left diagonals. Treat each parallel line as a stripe carrying its observed color. Collect every distinct observed stripe invariant and color, then infer the smallest period that assigns a single color to each residue; color every cell by its stripe residue, filling zeros. Use all observed lines: ignoring one can produce a false checkerboard or an unnecessarily large period. Validated examples include an 18x18 input with color 5 on r-c=5 and color 6 on r-c=10, completed as alternating diagonal stripes, and other demonstrations of full periodic tilings from diagonal samples. In a validated 27x27 case, down-right stripes at c-r=6 had color 5, while c-r=16 and c-r=-7 had color 1. The smallest consistent period was 3, with residues 0->5, 1->1, and 2->1; the correct output colored every cell by this residue. With only two observed residues, the period may be underdetermined; favor the simplest consistent motif or request a demonstration if its cost is justified. Validate that every observed stripe retains its color and preserve dimensions.","path":"general","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persist the correctly validated period-3 rule and prevent recurrence of the earlier incorrect period-2 and overlarge-period guesses.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_periodic_diagonal_stripe_patterns_across_the_grid","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend periodic diagonal stripe patterns across the grid","content":"For ARC grids whose nonzero cells lie on one or more complete/partial parallel diagonal lines and the output is a dense periodic pattern, identify the line-family invariant: r-c for down-right diagonals, or r+c for down-left diagonals. Treat each parallel line as a stripe carrying its observed color. Infer the smallest stripe-coordinate period consistent with the samples, then color every cell by its stripe residue, filling zeros. Example validated here: an 18x18 input had color 5 on r-c=5 and color 6 on r-c=10; the correct completion was alternating diagonal stripes, color 5 on odd r-c and color 6 on even r-c (period 2). Related demos showed full 2D periodic tilings can be recovered from diagonal samples: e.g. a period-3 r+c motif and period-5 c-r motif. Validate the output against every observed cell and preserve dimensions. With only two stripe samples, the period can be underdetermined; favor the simplest consistent motif, and request a demo if the cost is justified.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:23:53.214060+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:23:53.214077+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_periodic_diagonal_stripe_patterns_across_the_grid","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend periodic diagonal stripe patterns across the grid","content":"For ARC grids whose nonzero cells lie on one or more complete or partial parallel diagonal lines and whose output is a dense periodic pattern, identify the line-family invariant: c-r for down-right diagonals, or r+c for down-left diagonals. Treat each parallel line as a stripe carrying its observed color. Collect every distinct observed stripe invariant and color, then infer the smallest period that assigns a single color to each residue; color every cell by its stripe residue, filling zeros. Use all observed lines: ignoring one can produce a false checkerboard or an unnecessarily large period. Validated examples include an 18x18 input with color 5 on r-c=5 and color 6 on r-c=10, completed as alternating diagonal stripes, and other demonstrations of full periodic tilings from diagonal samples. In a validated 27x27 case, down-right stripes at c-r=6 had color 5, while c-r=16 and c-r=-7 had color 1. The smallest consistent period was 3, with residues 0->5, 1->1, and 2->1; the correct output colored every cell by this residue. With only two observed residues, the period may be underdetermined; favor the simplest consistent motif or request a demonstration if its cost is justified. Validate that every observed stripe retains its color and preserve dimensions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:23:53.214060+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:57:18.650Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
