tify the stripe family: c-r is constant for down-right diagonals; r+c is constant for anti-diagonals. Use all observed stripe anchors, including repeated anchors of the same color, to infer the smallest consistent period and the color assigned to each residue; do not infer a period from only one pair when other anchors provide constraints. Extend the inferred stripe coloring across the full grid while preserving its dimensions. Validated anti-diagonal example: in a 24x24 demo, color 7 anchors had sums r+c=18 and 32, while color 4 had sum 43. These imply period 3, with residue 1 colored 4 and residues 0 and 2 colored 7. In the corresponding 2x2 test, the observed cells had color 9 on sums 0 and 2 and color 3 on sum 1; fill the blank at sum 1 with 3, yielding [[9,3],[3,9]]. Check all anchor coordinates and resulting dimensions before submitting.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:23:53.214060+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:59:58.115Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130352350","summary":"Updated the global memory for periodic full-width row patterns with the validated anchor-count period rule.","rationale":"The demonstrated task has five non-background full-width row anchors, and both the demonstration output and correctly solved test assign anchor colors to row-index residues modulo five, then repeat those colors throughout the grid.","expectedOutcome":"For matching ARC tasks, identify full-width uniform colored row anchors, use their count as the vertical period, map each anchor color to its row-index residue, and fill every row by repeating those residue colors; validate the residue assignments and output dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_complete_periodic_uniform_row_color_patterns_from_sparse_row_anchors","title":"ARC: complete periodic uniform-row color patterns from sparse row anchors","content":"For ARC grids that are otherwise blank and contain sparse full-width uniform colored rows, identify only those full-width colored rows as anchors. Let the number of anchors be the vertical period p. Assign each anchor's color to its row-index residue modulo p, then repeat those residue colors down the entire grid. Preserve repeated colors when different residues map to the same color; output no blank rows and preserve the grid dimensions and width. Validate residue assignments against all anchors before filling. Example: five anchors at rows 2, 3, 11, 14, and 20 give p=5 and mappings 2→9, 3→8, 1→2, 4→5, 0→3. Another validated case gives residues 0→9, 1→7, 2→2, 3→2, 4→8.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This is a reusable rule explicitly requested and supported by the demonstration and correctly solved test instance.","before":{"id":"arc_complete_periodic_uniform_row_color_patterns_from_sparse_row_anchors","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: complete periodic uniform-row color patterns from sparse row anchors","content":"For ARC grids that are otherwise blank and contain a few full-width uniform colored rows, treat each full row as an anchor in a vertically periodic row-color sequence. Infer the smallest positive period p such that no two anchors with different colors occupy the same row residue modulo p. Assign each residue a color from its anchor(s), then fill all rows with that color across the full width, repeating vertically. Determine phase directly from absolute row indices (0-based); do not simply repeat the listed anchor colors in their order or preserve large blank gaps. Examples: anchors at rows 4:3, 6:7, 17:9, 23:1 imply minimum p=4 and residue sequence [3,9,7,1]; a demonstrated 13-row case had minimum p=7, and a 9-row case minimum p=5. Verify every anchor is preserved and output dimensions match input. Apply only when input consists of full-width row anchors on a blank background and a consistent repeating row pattern is supported.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:58:54.240592+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:59:19.987263+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_complete_periodic_uniform_row_color_patterns_from_sparse_row_anchors","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: complete periodic uniform-row color patterns from sparse row anchors","content":"For ARC grids that are otherwise blank and contain sparse full-width uniform colored rows, identify only those full-width colored rows as anchors. Let the number of anchors be the vertical period p. Assign each anchor's color to its row-index residue modulo p, then repeat those residue colors down the entire grid. Preserve repeated colors when different residues map to the same color; output no blank rows and preserve the grid dimensions and width. Validate residue assignments against all anchors before filling. Example: five anchors at rows 2, 3, 11, 14, and 20 give p=5 and mappings 2→9, 3→8, 1→2, 4→5, 0→3. Another validated case gives residues 0→9, 1→7, 2→2, 3→2, 4→8.","path":"arc","scope":"g