{"id":"refine_20260929131127103","summary":"Strengthened the global ARC memory for restoring rectangular patches using a confirmed demonstration and correctly solved test.","rationale":"The trajectory supports a reusable rule: multiple solid, axis-aligned rectangles can coexist with different backgrounds, and isolated anomalous cells may occur both inside and outside those rectangles. The task was solved correctly with no wrong submissions.","expectedOutcome":"For matching tasks, infer rectangle bounds and colors from aligned intact runs and whole-shape evidence, repair confirmed interior defects, remove confirmed exterior noise, and preserve all unrelated cells and dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with evidence of solid, axis-aligned rectangular color patches and isolated anomalous cells, infer each rectangle's bounds and color from aligned intact runs and whole-rectangle consistency. Multiple patch colors and rectangles can coexist; do not assume one foreground color or one background color. Rectangles may touch or share row/column alignments, so use the full shape evidence to distinguish patch cells from stray noise. Replace confirmed anomalous cells inside a rectangle with that rectangle's color, and replace confirmed isolated non-background noise outside supported rectangles with the inferred background. Preserve all unaffected cells and the original dimensions. Validate every proposed correction against the inferred bounds and the remaining intact portions before submitting. Confirmed example: a demonstration with background 0 and solid patches in colors 2, 6, and 7 contained isolated noise in color 5; the test used background 1, patches in colors 2 and 6, and several anomalous colors. The test was solved correctly with no wrong submissions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reaso