s cells inside a rectangle with that rectangle's color, and replace confirmed isolated non-background noise outside supported rectangles with the inferred background. Preserve all unaffected cells and the original dimensions. Validate every proposed correction against the inferred bounds and the remaining intact portions before submitting. Confirmed example: a demonstration with background 0 and solid patches in colors 2, 6, and 7 contained isolated noise in color 5; the test used background 1, patches in colors 2 and 6, and several anomalous colors. The test was solved correctly with no wrong submissions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:44.863Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:11:35.599Z","version":5},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929131858881","summary":"Strengthen the global ARC rectangle-repair memory with the demonstrated noise-handling rule and safeguards against speculative boundary changes.","rationale":"Seven demonstrations show solid axis-aligned rectangular patches on a uniform background, with arbitrary-color noise inside and outside; one example also replaces an interior patch cell with the background color. The active task repeatedly failed after unsupported rectangle extensions and once produced malformed grids, so cautious boundary inference and programmatic validation are warranted.","expectedOutcome":"For matching ARC tasks, restore only well-supported rectangle interiors, including confirmed cells obscured by background-colored noise, and clear confirmed exterior stray cells. Validate dimensions and changed cells programmatically before submission; avoid unsupported extensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with a dominant uniform background, solid axis-aligned rectangular color patches, and isolated anomalous cells, infer each patch's color and bounds from strong evidence such as aligned intact runs, repeated row or column spans, and whole-rectangle consistency. Repair confirmed interior defects to the patch color, including cells overwritten by arbitrary colors or occasionally by the background; clear confirmed stray non-background cells outside patches to the dominant background. Do not treat every nearby marker as evidence that a rectangle extends to it: an adjacent stray can be noise, and ambiguous edges should remain unchanged unless other aligned evidence confirms the extent. Demonstrations in this task family show multiple rectangles of different colors and sizes, with noise both inside and outside. Before submission, construct the result programmatically, verify the exact input dimensions and every row width, and compare changed cells against the inferred rectangle mask so only supported repairs and noise removals are made. If boundaries remain genuinely ambiguous, seek another demonstration rather than making speculative edits.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds evidence-backed handling for background-colored interior defects and emphasizes conservative boundary inference and grid validation after repeated speculative and malformed submissions.","before":{"id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","kind":"memory","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with evidence of solid, axis-aligned rectangular color patches and isolated anomalous cells, infer each rectangle's bounds and color from aligned intact runs and whole-rectangle consistency. Multiple patch colors and rectangles can coexist; do not assume one foreground color or one background color. Rectangles may touch or share row/column alignments, so use the full shape evidence to distinguish patch cells from stray noise. Replace confirmed anomalous cells inside a rectangle with that rectangle's color, and replace confirmed isolated non-background noise outside supported rectangles with the inferred background. Preserve all unaffected cells and the original dimensions. Validate every proposed correction against the inferred bounds and the remaining intact portions before submitting. Confirmed example: a demonstration with background 0 and solid patches in colors 2, 6, and 7 contained isolated noise in color 5; the test used background 1, patches in colors 2 and 6, and several anomalous colors. The test was solved correctly with no wrong submissions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:44.863Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:11:35.599Z","version":5},"after":{"id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","kind":"memory","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with a dominant uniform background, solid axis-aligned rectangular color patches, and isolated anomalous cells, infer each patch's color and bounds from strong evidence such as aligned intact runs, repeated row or column spans, and whole-rectangle consistency. Repair confirmed interior defects to the patch color, including cells overwritten by arbitrary colors or occasionally by the background; clear confirmed stray non-background cells outside patches to the dominant background. Do not treat every nearby marker as evidence that a rectangle extends to it: an adjacent stray can be noise, and ambiguous edges should remain unchanged unless other aligned evidence confirms the extent. Demonstrations in this task family show multiple rectangles of different colors and sizes, with noise both inside and outside. Before submission, construct the result programmatically, verify the exact input dimensions and every row width, and compare changed cells against the inferred rectangle mask so only supported repairs and noise removals are made. If boundaries remain genuinely ambiguous, seek another demonstration rather than making speculative edits.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:44.863Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:19:06.525Z","version":6},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132108396","summary":"Strengthened the global ARC rectangle-repair memory with a whole-grid scan and validation safeguard.","rationale":"The task’s demonstrations supported restoring rectangular patches and removing isolated anomalies. Two incorrect submissions resulted from overlooking a small, separate 2×2 color-6 patch at rows 2–3, columns 17–18; adding a systematic scan for small and edge-adjacent patches is directly supported by that failure.","expectedOutcome":"For matching tasks, the agent should identify and repair all well-supported rectangular patches, including small separated ones, while clearing only confirmed noise. Validate the complete output row by row against the inferred patch bounds and preserve unrelated cells and dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with a dominant uniform background, solid axis-aligned rectangular color patches, and isolated anomalous cells, infer each patch's color and bounds from strong evidence such as aligned intact runs, repeated row or column spans, and the demonstrations. Restore only confirmed patch cells obscured by anomalies, including background-colored defects inside a patch; clear only confirmed exterior stray markers. Do not speculate about extending patch boundaries or changing unrelated background cells. Scan the entire grid systematically, including far-right and bottom regions and small separated rectangles: a genuine 2×2 patch can be easy to miss even when larger patches are apparent. Use the demonstrations to distinguish patch colors and shapes from noise. Before submitting, verify dimensions and inspect every row against the inferred patch bounds to ensure all supported patches are restored, exterior noise is removed, and all unrelated cells remain unchanged.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"In a solved instance, a small separate 2×2 color-6 patch at rows 2–3, columns 17–18 was missed in the first candidates and required a whole-grid scan; the demonstrations supported the existing patch-repair rule.","before":{"id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","kind":"memory","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with a dominant uniform background, solid axis-aligned rectangular color patches, and isolated anomalous cells, infer each patch's color and bounds from strong evidence such as aligned intact runs, repeated row or column spans, and whole-rectangle consistency. Repair confirmed interior defects to the patch color, including cells overwritten by arbitrary colors or occasionally by the background; clear confirmed stray non-background cells outside patches to the dominant background. Do not treat every nearby marker as evidence that a rectangle extends to it: an adjacent stray can be noise, and ambiguous edges should remain unchanged unless other aligned evidence confirms the extent. Demonstrations in this task family show multiple rectangles of different colors and sizes, with noise both inside and outside. Before submission, construct the result programmatically, verify the exact input dimensions and every row width, and compare changed cells against the inferred rectangle mask so only supported repairs and noise removals are made. If boundaries remain genuinely ambiguous, seek another demonstration rather than making speculative edits.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:44.863Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:19:06.525Z","version":6},"after":{"id":"arc_restore_rectangular_patches_and_remove_isolated_noise","kind":"memory","title":"Restore rectangular patches and remove isolated noise","content":"For ARC grids with a dominant uniform background, solid axis-aligned rectangular color patches, and isolated anomalous cells, infer each patch's color and bounds from strong evidence such as aligned intact runs, repeated row or column spans, and the demonstrations. Restore only confirmed patch cells obscured by anomalies, including background-colored defects inside a patch; clear only confirmed exterior stray markers. Do not speculate about extending patch boundaries or changing unrelated background cells. Scan the entire grid systematically, including far-right and bottom regions and small separated rectangles: a genuine 2×2 patch can be easy to miss even when larger patches are apparent. Use the demonstrations to distinguish patch colors and shapes from noise. Before submitting, verify dimensions and inspect every row against the inferred patch bounds to ensure all supported patches are restored, exterior noise is removed, and all unrelated cells remain unchanged.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:44.863Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:21:14.861Z","version":7},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132242703","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for extending clearly anchored same-color diagonal rays.","rationale":"The demonstration and solved test both support continuing a same-color diagonal through successive cells in the established direction, while preserving other grid contents; this is distinct from inferring periodic diagonal stripes.","expectedOutcome":"For similar tasks, continue only a clearly evidenced diagonal ray to the grid edge, preserving existing cells and unrelated objects; validate the direction and output dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_continue_aligned_same_color_diagonal_ray","title":"Continue a clearly anchored same-color diagonal ray","content":"For ARC grids with two clearly aligned same-color cells on a diagonal, continue that same-color line one cell at a time in the established direction until the grid edge. Preserve all existing cells and unrelated objects. Extend only the evidenced ray direction; do not infer additional parallel diagonals or a periodic stripe pattern without separate evidence. This rule is distinct from periodic diagonal-stripe completion, which requires evidence for a repeating stripe family. Validate that the anchors agree on the diagonal direction, that only the ray cells were added, and that the grid dimensions remain unchanged.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A demonstration and correctly solved test independently showed the same rule: aligned same-color diagonal anchors continued to the boundary.","after":{"id":"arc_continue_aligned_same_color_diagonal_ray","kind":"memory","title":"Continue a clearly anchored same-color diagonal ray","content":"For ARC grids with two clearly aligned same-color cells on a diagonal, continue that same-color line one cell at a time in the established direction until the grid edge. Preserve all existing cells and unrelated objects. Extend only the evidenced ray direction; do not infer additional parallel diagonals or a periodic stripe pattern without separate evidence. This rule is distinct from periodic diagonal-stripe completion, which requires evidence for a repeating stripe family. Validate that the anchors agree on the diagonal direction, that only the ray cells were added, and that the grid dimensions remain unchanged.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:22:47.884Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:22:47.884Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132452787","summary":"Updated the global diagonal-ray memory with validated blocker and preservation rules.","rationale":"The task was solved by extending a same-color diagonal pair through background cells, stopping when the ray reached a different non-background color; the demonstrations also support leaving grids without a same-color diagonal pair unchanged.","expectedOutcome":"For matching ARC tasks, extend only clearly anchored same-color diagonal rays, stop at grid edges or occupied cells, preserve existing markers and dimensions, and avoid changes when no qualifying pair exists.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays until blocked","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend a diagonal ray when two or more cells of the same non-background color lie on that diagonal (row and column each change by ±1). Continue from the aligned anchors in the established direction through background cells, stopping at the grid edge or immediately before any occupied non-background cell; do not overwrite that blocker or any existing cells. Preserve unrelated markers and grid dimensions. If no same-color diagonal pair establishes a ray, leave the grid unchanged. Validate row/column indexing, slope, blocker positions, and output dimensions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A correctly solved instance and demonstrations validate extending anchored rays while treating other colors as blockers and preserving existing markers.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend clear nonzero diagonal color rays until blocked","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, when two or more same-color nonzero cells lie on a diagonal (step ±1 row, ±1 column), extend that diagonal ray through background cells in both directions. Stop at the grid boundary or just before an existing non-background cell of any color; preserve all existing cells rather than overwrite them. Apply independently to each clearly aligned color pair. Demonstrated: a 9 diagonal was extended to the edge; a 2 diagonal extended backward to the edge and stopped at a different-color cell. In the solved test, 7 and 6 diagonal rays were extended into background, while their continuations were stopped by existing cells; other diagonal continuation was blocked at once. Safeguard: do not extrapolate a mere diagonal pair of color 0 without additional evidence (a demo showed a 0 pair left unchanged). Validate both orientations, both ends, blockers, and grid dimensions.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286462+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays until blocked","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend a diagonal ray when two or more cells of the same non-background color lie on that diagonal (row and column each change by ±1). Continue from the aligned anchors in the established direction through background cells, stopping at the grid edge or immediately before any occupied non-background cell; do not overwrite that blocker or any existing cells. Preserve unrelated markers and grid dimensions. If no same-color diagonal pair establishes a ray, leave the grid unchanged. Validate row/column indexing, slope, blocker positions, and output dimensions.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:59.482Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132725904","summary":"Strengthen the global diagonal-ray memory with the confirmed rule to extend exposed same-color diagonal ends to the edge or a blocker.","rationale":"The solved task and demonstrations show continuous extension from an exposed endpoint, not a one-cell extension. In the test, the 3 ray extends up-right to the edge and is blocked down-left by a 6; the 5 ray extends down-right to the edge. Prior submissions failed by stopping early or omitting an exposed ray.","expectedOutcome":"For matching sparse diagonal-ray puzzles, extend each evidenced exposed end continuously to the boundary or first occupied cell, preserve all original cells, and avoid extending unrelated repeated pairs. Validate coordinates, blockers, and dimensions before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays to the edge or blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend a clearly anchored same-color diagonal ray continuously from each exposed endpoint until the grid edge or the first occupied cell. Use at least two aligned same-color cells to establish the diagonal direction (row and column each change by ±1). If one end joins or is blocked by a multicolor source object, do not continue through it; extend the other exposed end away from that object. Preserve all original cells and unrelated objects. Do not extend vertical or horizontal repeated pairs merely because they repeat; require the demonstrated diagonal-ray pattern. In a confirmed example, color 3 extended up-right from its exposed endpoint to the edge while its down-left continuation was blocked by color 6; color 5 extended down-right to the edge. Earlier errors came from extending only one cell or omitting an exposed ray. Validate each ray’s direction, bounds, blockers, and output dimensions before submission.","metadata"