{"id":"refine_20260929132921794","summary":"Create a global ARC memory for extending the exposed diagonal ray of same-color markers in a sparse multicolor motif.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly, and three demonstrations plus the test support a reusable rule: extend a clearly established same-color diagonal outward from its exposed endpoint, using the surrounding motif to choose the direction rather than extending both ends indiscriminately.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, identify the intended diagonal direction from marker alignment and local motif structure, extend only the supported exposed ray while preserving existing cells, and validate bounds and blockers.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_exposed_diagonal_ray_from_multicolor_motif","title":"ARC: extend the exposed diagonal ray from a sparse multicolor motif","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and a sparse multicolor motif, inspect aligned same-color diagonal markers and their local relationships to the other motif cells. When a same-color diagonal run is clearly established, extend its ray outward from the structurally exposed endpoint, preserving all original cells and stopping at the grid edge or the first occupied non-background cell. Use the surrounding motif to distinguish the intended exposed direction; do not extend both ends blindly or treat every repeated pair as a ray. Demonstrations validated rays of colors 7 and 6, and rays of colors 2 and 9 extending away from neighboring motif colors. A solved test had background 1: color 9 at (r1,c3) and (r2,c4) extended up-left from its exposed end; color 7 at (r3,c4) and (r4,c3) extended down-left; and color 6 at (r3,c5) and (r4,c6) extended down-right. Preserve dimensions and unrelated cells, and verify the chosen direction and each changed coordinate before submission.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Persists a validated, reusable diagonal-ray rule that selects the direction 