{"id":"refine_20260929132947302","summary":"Update the global diagonal-ray memory with a confirmed example of extending an up-right ray while preserving an unrelated blocker.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly: two aligned 9 markers at (6,2) and (7,1) supported extending the up-right diagonal to the top edge. The output preserved existing and unrelated cells, including the occupied cell at (8,0).","expectedOutcome":"For similar sparse-grid tasks, extend only a clearly evidenced same-color diagonal, preserve unrelated cells, and respect occupied cells at the ray's exposed ends; validate the direction and bounds before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays to the edge or blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend only clearly evidenced same-color diagonal rays from their exposed endpoints, one cell at a time along the established direction. Continue through empty cells to the grid edge or stop before an occupied non-background cell. A confirmed case had 9 markers at (row 6, col 2) and (row 7, col 1), establishing an up-right ray that was extended through empty cells to the top edge. Preserve all original and unrelated cells; the occupied cell at (row 8, col 0) remained unchanged and blocked the opposite down-left continuation. Do not invent additional rays from unrelated markers. Validate the coordinates, direction, blockers, and unchanged grid dimensions before submitting.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Add a confirmed example to the existing reusable diagonal-ray rule without broadening it beyond clearly anchored rays.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays to the edge or blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend a clearly evidenced same-color diagonal ray outward from its exposed endpoint(s), following the established diagonal one cell at a time until the grid edge or the first occupied non-background cell. A pair of same-color cells on a diagonal can establish the ray direction; do not extend unrelated repeated pairs speculatively. Preserve all original cells and blockers, and retain the grid dimensions. A validated case had separate two-cell diagonal rays of colors 1 and 4: each ray's outward upper path was blocked by a different occupied cell (colors 9 and 7), while the opposite exposed end continued to the grid edge. Validate ray alignment, endpoint directions, blockers, and dimensions before submitting.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:28:25.903Z","version":4},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays to the edge or blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend only clearly evidenced same-color diagonal rays from their exposed endpoints, one cell at a time along the established direction. Continue through empty cells to the grid edge or stop before an occupied non-background cell. A confirmed case had 9 markers at (row 6, col 2) and (row 7, col 1), establishing an up-right ray that was extended through empty cells to the top edge. Preserve all original and unrelated cells; the occupied cell at (row 8, col 0) remained unchanged and blocked the opposite down-left continuation. Do not invent additional rays from unrelated markers. Validate the coordinates, direction, blockers, and unchanged grid dimensions before submitting.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:29:55.623Z","version":5},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133053126","summary":"Strengthen the global diagonal-ray memory with the validated rule to extend established rays in both directions and stop at occupied cells.","rationale":"The solved task confirms bidirectional extension from aligned same-color markers, with other-color cells acting as blockers. The first attempt extended a ray in only one direction and was incorrect; the demonstrations also support stopping at occupied cells.","expectedOutcome":"For matching sparse-grid tasks, extend each clearly evidenced diagonal run from its exposed endpoints in both directions, stopping at grid edges or occupied non-background cells while preserving original cells and blockers.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays in both directions to the edge or a blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, identify clearly evidenced same-color diagonal runs (at least two aligned cells) and extend continuously outward from each exposed endpoint in both directions along that diagonal. Stop at the grid edge or immediately before the first occupied non-background cell; preserve all original cells and blockers, including blockers of another color. Do not extend only one direction by default, and do not infer rays from unrelated repeated pairs without clear alignment and task evidence. In a validated example, a diagonal of color 1 extended up-left to the edge but stopped down-right at a color-4 cell; a color-4 diagonal extended up-right and down-left to the edges. Demonstrations also confirm stopping at occupied cells. Validate ray direction, endpoints, blockers, and dimensions before submission.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"This sharpens the existing diagonal-ray memory with a solved case and records the failed one-direction interpretation to prevent repeating it.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays to the edge or blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, extend only clearly evidenced same-color diagonal rays from their exposed endpoints, one cell at a time along the established direction. Continue through empty cells to the grid edge or stop before an occupied non-background cell. A confirmed case had 9 markers at (row 6, col 2) and (row 7, col 1), establishing an up-right ray that was extended through empty cells to the top edge. Preserve all original and unrelated cells; the occupied cell at (row 8, col 0) remained unchanged and blocked the opposite down-left continuation. Do not invent additional rays from unrelated markers. Validate the coordinates, direction, blockers, and unchanged grid dimensions before submitting.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:29:55.623Z","version":5},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_clear_nonzero_diagonal_color_rays_until_blocked","kind":"memory","title":"Extend anchored same-color diagonal rays in both directions to the edge or a blocker","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored pixels, identify clearly evidenced same-color diagonal runs (at least two aligned cells) and extend continuously outward from each exposed endpoint in both directions along that diagonal. Stop at the grid edge or immediately before the first occupied non-background cell; preserve all original cells and blockers, including blockers of another color. Do not extend only one direction by default, and do not infer rays from unrelated repeated pairs without clear alignment and task evidence. In a validated example, a diagonal of color 1 extended up-left to the edge but stopped down-right at a color-4 cell; a color-4 diagonal extended up-right and down-left to the edges. Demonstrations also confirm stopping at occupied cells. Validate ray direction, endpoints, blockers, and dimensions before submission.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:24:13.286444+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:31:01.290Z","version":6},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133303451","summary":"Created a global ARC memory for the demonstrated four-rotation grid expansion.","rationale":"Three demonstrations and a correctly solved test support a consistent rule: place the input and its three quarter-turn rotations in fixed quadrants of a doubled-size output.","expectedOutcome":"For matching square-grid tasks with a doubled-size output, the agent can construct the four quadrants in the demonstrated order and validate dimensions and orientation.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_expand_grid_into_four_rotations","title":"ARC: expand a square grid into four rotated quadrants","content":"For ARC tasks where the demonstrated output is twice the input's height and width, and the input is square, divide the output into four equal quadrants. Put the original input in the top-left, its 90-degree clockwise rotation in the top-right, its 90-degree counterclockwise rotation in the bottom-left, and its 180-degree rotation in the bottom-right. Assemble rows by concatenating the corresponding top quadrants, then the corresponding bottom quadrants. This fixed arrangement was confirmed by demonstrations with 2×2, 9×9, and 12×12 inputs and correctly solved a 4×4 test. Apply only when the examples support this transformation; preserve cell values and verify each quadrant's orientation and the final 2n×2n dimensions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The rule is reusable and was corroborated across multiple demonstrations and a correct test submission.","after":{"id":"arc_expand_grid_into_four_rotations","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: expand a square grid into four rotated quadrants","content":"For ARC tasks where the demonstrated output is twice the input's height and width, and the input is square, divide the output into four equal quadrants. Put the original input in the top-left, its 90-degree clockwise rotation in the top-right, its 90-degree counterclockwise rotation in the bottom-left, and its 180-degree rotation in the bottom-right. Assemble rows by concatenating the corresponding top quadrants, then the corresponding bottom quadrants. This fixed arrangement was confirmed by demonstrations with 2×2, 9×9, and 12×12 inputs and correctly solved a 4×4 test. Apply only when the examples support this transformation; preserve cell values and verify each quadrant's orientation and the final 2n×2n dimensions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:33:10.460Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:33:10.460Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
