{"id":"refine_20260929134012294","summary":"Create a global ARC memory for converting marker counts in separator-defined panels into an output grid.","rationale":"The task’s four demonstrations and correctly solved test support a reusable rule: partition the grid at full-color separators, count rare markers within each equal-sized panel, and emit one cell per panel; the test also exposed the need to use the panel background color rather than the marker color for singleton panels.","expectedOutcome":"For matching ARC tasks, identify the panel background, separator, and marker colors; preserve panel order; and map a panel with exactly one marker to the background color and other demonstrated counts to color 1. Validate panel counts and output dimensions before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_encode_marker_counts_in_separator_panels","title":"ARC: encode marker counts in separator-defined panels","content":"For ARC grids partitioned into equal-sized panels by full-width separator rows and/or full-height separator columns, identify the dominant panel background, separator color, and rare marker color. Exclude separator lines, count markers independently within each panel, and emit one output cell per panel in the same row-major arrangement. Across the demonstrated tasks, a panel with exactly one marker maps to the panel background color; panels with zero or multiple markers map to color 1. The demonstrated cases include backgrounds 6, 7, 8, and 3, so use the actual background color rather than the marker color. Before submitting, verify each panel boundary and count, and confirm the output dimensions equal the number of panel rows by panel columns.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Four demonstrations and the correctly solved test support this panel-count transformation; an earlier wrong answer used the marker color instead of the background color for singleton panels.","after":{"id":"arc_encode_marker_counts_in_separator_panels","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: encode marker counts in separator-defined panels","content":"For ARC grids partitioned into equal-sized panels by full-width separator rows and/or full-height separator columns, identify the dominant panel background, separator color, and rare marker color. Exclude separator lines, count markers independently within each panel, and emit one output cell per panel in the same row-major arrangement. Across the demonstrated tasks, a panel with exactly one marker maps to the panel background color; panels with zero or multiple markers map to color 1. The demonstrated cases include backgrounds 6, 7, 8, and 3, so use the actual background color rather than the marker color. Before submitting, verify each panel boundary and count, and confirm the output dimensions equal the number of panel rows by panel columns.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:40:26.295Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:40:26.295Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/attempts/9c086a91621049f09b6809851224e395/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929134214574","summary":"Updated the global memory with the validated exactly-two-marker rule and the test’s panel layout and output.","rationale":"The task confirmed that full separator rows and columns define panels, each panel is mapped to one output cell, and only panels with exactly two marker cells receive 1; other panels retain the background color. The demo’s actual layout was 5×4 panels, while the test’s layout was 3×5.","expectedOutcome":"Future matching tasks should count every marker within each panel, preserve panel order and background values for nonmatching counts, and produce an output with one row and column per panel.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_encode_exactly_two_marker_panels_as_1","title":"ARC: encode panels with exactly two markers as 1","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by full-width and/or full-height uniform separator lines into equal rectangular panels, identify the panel background, separator color, and marker color. Exclude separator lines; count every marker cell inside each panel. Output one cell per panel in the original panel row/column order: use 1 if and only if that panel contains exactly two markers, and otherwise use the panel background color. Output dimensions equal the number of panel rows by panel columns. Validate panel bounds and counts carefully before submitting. Confirmed example: a 19×19 grid with separator rows and columns formed a 5×4 panel layout and yielded a 5×4 output. Confirmed test: separators at rows 3 and 7 and columns 2, 5, 8, and 11 formed a 3×5 panel layout; with background 7, the correct output was [[7,1,1,7,1],[1,7,7,7,1],[1,1,7,1,1]].","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves the reusable rule and adds a correctly counted, validated example while clarifying panel dimensions.","before":{"id":"arc_encode_exactly_two_marker_panels_as_1","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: encode exactly-two marker panels as 1","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by full-width/full-height uniform separator lines into equal rectangular panels, count the non-background, non-separator marker cells in each panel. Output one cell per panel in the same panel layout: use 1 if that panel contains exactly two markers, otherwise use the panel background color. Ignore separator lines. This rule was confirmed across two demonstrations with different backgrounds, separator colors, and marker colors, and solved a test with a 2x3 panel layout.","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:41:35.550913+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:41:35.550933+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_encode_exactly_two_marker_panels_as_1","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: encode panels with exactly two markers as 1","content":"For ARC grids partitioned by full-width and/or full-height uniform separator lines into equal rectangular panels, identify the panel background, separator color, and marker color. Exclude separator lines; count every marker cell inside each panel. Output one cell per panel in the original panel row/column order: use 1 if and only if that panel contains exactly two markers, and otherwise use the panel background color. Output dimensions equal the number of panel rows by panel columns. Validate panel bounds and counts carefully before submitting. Confirmed example: a 19×19 grid with separator rows and columns formed a 5×4 panel layout and yielded a 5×4 output. Confirmed test: separators at rows 3 and 7 and columns 2, 5, 8, and 11 formed a 3×5 panel layout; with background 7, the correct output was [[7,1,1,7,1],[1,7,7,7,1],[1,1,7,1,1]].","path":"arc","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:41:35.550913+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:42:23.953Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682233/a